  "dataCard": {},
  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/pyrrhulina_cf_brevis_red_spots_en/",
      "html": "<p>Despite the fact that the males are quarrelling quite a lot, these quarrels are harmless and don't even lead to fin damage.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/pyrrhulina_cf_brevis_red_spots_en/",
      "html": "<p>When the males came into breeding mood in the photo tank and started to quarrel, they became even more colourful. At a glance, deep red spots developed on the scales, which were hardly visible before. Females don't get these spots at all.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/pyrrhulina_cf_brevis_red_spots_en/",
      "html": "<p>Lexicon: Pyrrhulina means \"little bullfinch\". brevis: Latin, means \"short\".</p><p>Suggestion of a common name: Red Spotted Pyrrhulina.</p>"
    }
